    <section id="tabs" aria-labelledby="h-tabs">
      <span class="eyebrow">Everyone</span>
      <h2 id="h-tabs">The seven tabs, plus the action calendar</h2>
      <div class="tabs-grid">
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Routines <small>daily · weekly · monthly · quarterly</small></h3><p>The recurring work for every brand, from the Reddit report to the quarterly access review. Each routine links straight to the tool it needs.</p><p class="tip">Tick it the moment you finish. The tick shows who did it and when. Routines assigned to you also appear in your column on Tasks when they’re due. They’re marked <b>→ You</b>.</p><p class="tip"><b>Brand by brand:</b> a routine an admin set to <b>Tick off each brand separately</b> shows a button per brand. Tick each brand as you finish it; the routine is done when all are, and the morning summary lists the brands still to do.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Tasks <small>the main board</small></h3><p>A column per person with their open tasks and the routines due for them now. Tasks are colored by brand; routines are purple with a dashed edge. Urgent work goes on the <b>Hotlist</b> and pops up once a day for its owner. Finished tasks stay crossed out below for 14 days. To look further back, pick a <b>From</b> and <b>To</b> date there, or a quick range like <b>Last month</b>.</p><p class="tip"><b>Results:</b> when you finish a task, an <b>Add result</b> box opens under Finished. Paste the link to what it produced (the post, page, email or listing) and a few words. Results show on the task, on a launch’s page and in the Monthly report’s <b>What we shipped</b>.</p><p class="tip">Add a task right in anyone’s column (yours included): click <b>Add a task…</b>, type it, and the brand and due date appear; press Add. <b>Mine</b> shows just your column. Point at a card to see its <b>Edit</b> link.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Sticky Notes <small>private</small></h3><p>Your personal sticky notes with check-off lists. Five colors. Nobody else sees them.</p><p class="tip">New notes float in the top right of every page until you press <b>Unstick</b>. Click a note's title to fold it away. <b>Stick to screen</b> on the tab brings it back.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3><span class="bi bi-md bi-slack" style="--bi:#4A154B" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="{{ $sprite }}#bi-slack"></use></svg></span>Slack <small>live channels</small></h3><p>Recent messages from team channels like #reddit-monitor, with a reply box. Posts show as “Your name (via OmniVEN)”. A red <b>*</b> means there's something new.</p><p class="tip">Leave it open while you work. It refreshes every minute.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Brands <small>8 hubs</small></h3><p>The <b>brand × platform grid</b> gives you one click to any brand's Instagram, LinkedIn, Meta Ads, Merchant Center and more. Each brand has a hub page with all of its accounts, tools and routines.</p><p class="tip">39DollarGlasses' {{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('klaviyo', 'xs') }}Klaviyo square opens the Klaviyo dashboard. A faded square has no direct link yet. A corner dot means access hasn't been checked in 90 days.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Tools <small>directory + bulletin</small></h3><p>Every marketing tool by category, the <b>Platform consoles</b> we open most (<span class="bi bi-xs bi-shopperapproved bi-multi" style="--bi:#E86600" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="{{ $sprite }}#bi-shopperapproved"></use></svg></span>Shopper Approved, our main review platform, is first), the <b>Affiliate</b> section with our two networks, {{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('cj', 'xs') }}CJ (Commission Junction) and {{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('awin', 'xs') }}Awin, the <b>Blog</b> section with {{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('contentful', 'xs') }}Contentful, where the 39DollarGlasses and Ocusafe blogs are written, and the team <b>Bulletin</b> for announcements.</p><p class="tip">The search box above the tabs searches all of OmniVEN, or press <kbd>⌘K</kbd> and type <b>cj</b>, <b>awin</b>, <b>klaviyo</b> or <b>contentful</b>.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('pulsetopost', 'xs') }}PulseToPost <small>strip above the tabs</small></h3><p>Today's numbers from PulseToPost, our research and drafting pipeline: stories <b>awaiting review</b> (click the number to open the review queue), <b>gated</b> stories in amber that need a medical or regulatory check, <b>drafts to review</b> and <b>spend this month</b>. The line under it says when the daily run last finished and how many stories it kept. It turns red after 26 hours without a run.</p><p class="tip">Approve and clear gates inside PulseToPost, so your name goes in its audit log. A <b>—</b> means PulseToPost couldn't count that figure. <b>Status unavailable</b> means OmniVEN couldn't reach it and shows when it last could, never old numbers. Admins also see why, for example a token that doesn’t match. Figures refresh every 5 minutes.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Sales <small>admins · top bar</small></h3><p>Revenue, orders, conversion rate and average order from Google Analytics 4, compared with the same dates last year. The <b>Sales</b> strip above the tabs shows yesterday, month to date and each brand. The <b>Sales</b> page breaks it down by channel (organic search, paid search, shopping, email, affiliate, <b>AI assistants</b> and more) and by brand, with each channel’s last 8 weeks.</p><p class="tip">Pick any dates (a preset like Last 90 days, Last year or All history, or your own From and To) and what to compare with: <b>Same dates last year</b>, the <b>Previous period</b> of the same length, or nothing. Every tile shows the change and the number then, and <b>Over time</b> charts revenue, orders, sessions, conversion or average order with the comparison as a dashed line (hover for values, or Show as table). History goes back to the first month GA4 has. Numbers refresh about every 30 minutes. GA4 usually counts a little less than the store does, so use it for trends and the channel mix.</p><p class="tip"><b>Goals:</b> admins set each brand’s revenue goal for the month under By brand. A bar then shows how much of the goal is in and whether that’s on pace for the share of the month gone (the tick mark). <b>Weekly review:</b> each week, write what moved and why at the bottom of Sales; the “Weekly revenue review” routine reminds you.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Campaign links <small>top bar · everyone</small></h3><p>The UTM link builder. Pick the brand, the page, where the link will be posted (Instagram, Klaviyo email, CJ, Awin, a partner site…) and a campaign name. OmniVEN adds the tags so the visit lands in the right channel on the Sales page, and keeps every link in a shared list.</p><p class="tip">Reuse campaign names from the suggestions so a campaign’s results add up. Tag every link you post outside the website, except Google Ads and Microsoft Ads, which tag themselves.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>AI answers <small>Jump to · monthly</small></h3><p>How often ChatGPT, Google’s AI Overview, Perplexity, Gemini and Copilot recommend or mention us when shoppers ask questions like “Where can I buy prescription safety glasses online?”. Each month, press <b>Ask ↗</b> on each question, see what the assistant says, and pick <b>Recommended us</b>, <b>Mentioned us</b> or <b>Not mentioned</b>. Note who they named instead.</p><p class="tip">Use a private or logged-out window so past chats don’t sway the answer. The monthly routine “Check what AI assistants say about us” links here. Admins add or retire questions at the bottom of the page.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('cj', 'xs') }}{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('awin', 'xs') }}Affiliate scoreboard <small>Sales → Affiliates · admins</small></h3><p>Affiliate sales, orders, commission and <b>pending commission</b> from CJ and Awin for any range of months, against the months before or the same months last year: each network side by side, the top 15 publishers, and month by month. <b>Cost of sale</b> is commission as a share of sales: the lower, the better value a publisher is.</p><p class="tip">Pick the dates at the top; totals are kept by month, so a range covers whole months. Up to five years of history load a few months each hour. Numbers refresh about every hour; pending amounts change as the networks approve them.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Reviews <small>top bar · everyone</small></h3><p>Star ratings for Shopper Approved, Trustpilot, Google, ConsumerAffairs and BBB, with the change over 30 days. Shopper Approved and Trustpilot update by themselves and list the last 30 days of reviews; every new review of <b>2★ or less</b> becomes a <b>Hotlist</b> task for Leona, due the next day, with the review and a link.</p><p class="tip">Google, ConsumerAffairs and BBB have no feed: press <b>Update rating</b> on their card when you check them. “Waiting on a reply” clears when the review’s task is ticked.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('klaviyo', 'xs') }}Email &amp; SMS <small>Sales → Email &amp; SMS · admins</small></h3><p><b>Over any dates</b> (up to five years back): emails received, open and click rates, orders and revenue credited to email and SMS, unsubscribes and new subscribers, split into campaigns and flows and compared with the previous period or last year, with a chart. Below that, the last 30 days in detail: buyers and (once order values are sent) revenue from each campaign and flow, open, click and unsubscribe rates, and the main list’s size and growth. Test sends under 500 people are left out.</p><p class="tip">A red <b>Fix</b> or amber <b>Watch</b> line at the top names anything hurting email results, such as order events without a value, a high unsubscribe or spam rate, or poor delivery.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Monthly report <small>Sales → Monthly report · admins</small></h3><p>One page per month for leadership: revenue against last year and the goal, each brand, the top channels, affiliates, ratings and reviews, AI answers, work finished by person and brand, launches, and the weekly notes. Sections without data are left out.</p><p class="tip">Use the month arrows to look back. <b>Print or save as PDF</b> gives a clean copy without the menus. The current month is marked “so far”.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('google', 'xs') }}Search <small>top bar · everyone</small></h3><p>Each brand’s Google Search Console for any dates in the last 16 months (all Search Console keeps), against the previous period or last year. By default, the last 28 days against the 28 before: clicks, impressions, click-through rate, average position, and <b>branded searches</b> (people looking for us by name, a good sign of awareness). Then the searches and pages <b>losing clicks</b>, biggest drops first, and the top searches.</p><p class="tip">Press <b>Make a task</b> on a falling search or page: the new-task form opens with the title and the numbers filled in. Search Console runs about three days behind.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Content gaps <small>Search → Content gaps · everyone</small></h3><p>A to-do list of content to write or fix, worked out from Search Console and the AI answers check:</p><ul><li><b>Few clicks</b>: we show in Google’s top 5 but few people click. Rewrite the title and description.</li><li><b>Almost there</b>: position 8–20. A stronger section on the page could reach the top 5.</li><li><b>Unanswered question</b>: people ask it and we aren’t in the top 10. Write an answer.</li><li><b>AI leaves us out</b>: no assistant mentioned us for that question in the latest monthly check.</li></ul><p class="tip">Pick who and when, then <b>Make a task</b>: the task comes with the steps. When the page is live, add its link as the task’s result. <b>Set aside</b> hides a gap for good; <b>Restore</b> under Recently handled brings it back.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Site check <small>Search → Site check · everyone</small></h3><p>Once a day OmniVEN reads the <b>structured data</b> on the pages listed there (the hidden labels that let Google show price, stock and stars) and says what’s missing, by kind of page. It also flags a page set to <b>noindex</b> or with a canonical tag pointing elsewhere. Red means Google can’t show rich results; amber means they could be better.</p><p>It also downloads each brand’s <b>Google Shopping product feed</b> and counts the problems Merchant Center rejects or weakens products for: missing price, image or stock, prices not like “39.00 USD”, duplicate ids, no GTIN or MPN, over-long titles. Only counts and a few example ids are kept.</p><p class="tip">Admins add pages at the bottom (start with <b>Add each brand’s home page</b>, then best-selling products, a category and a guide). <b>Make a task</b> on a page sends the fix list to the developer; check it after with Google’s Rich Results Test.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Reddit <small>top bar → Jump to · everyone</small></h3><p>Every hour OmniVEN searches Reddit (read-only) for threads from the past week that are worth a reply, best first: an <b>unhappy customer</b>, someone who <b>mentions us</b>, someone <b>asking for an alternative</b> to Zenni, Warby Parker and the like, and <b>buying questions</b> in the eyewear subreddits. Newer threads with a few comments rank higher.</p><p class="tip"><b>Make a task</b> gives it to someone, due today (complaints go on the Hotlist), with the link and how to reply. Always say you work for the brand, and check the subreddit’s rules on self-promotion. Add the link to your comment as the task’s result. <b>Skip</b> hides a thread; <b>Restore</b> brings it back.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Outreach <small>top bar → Jump to · everyone</small></h3><p>The blogs, review sites, affiliate publishers, creators and press we pitch, and where each one stands: <b>To contact → Contacted → Replied → Working it out → Live</b>, or Declined or No reply. Each has an owner, a follow-up date and a history.</p><p class="tip">Add one at a time or paste a list. On a publisher, log each step with one click: <b>Pitched them</b> or <b>Followed up</b> sets the next follow-up a week out, <b>They replied</b> gives you three days to answer, <b>It’s live</b> saves the link. Follow-ups that are due sit at the top of Outreach. Give a CJ or Awin publisher its network ID to see its affiliate sales (for people who can see revenue). Live placements show in the Monthly report.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Affiliate assets <small>top bar → Jump to · everyone</small></h3><p>The approved banners and images, discount codes, copy (short and long brand descriptions, product blurbs) and links (photo folders, landing pages) that affiliates, review sites and creators may use. Copy a code or text with one click; download a banner.</p><p class="tip">Admins add assets, with an optional end date (ended ones drop out). A code can belong to one publisher; it then shows on their Outreach page. <b>Share a kit</b> makes a link a publisher can open without signing in, for 7, 30 or 90 days. It leaves out staff-only assets, ended codes and other publishers’ codes.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Campaigns <small>top bar → Jump to · everyone</small></h3><p>A campaign is a named push with dates, like “Black Friday 2026”. Its page gathers its <b>tracked links</b> (every campaign link with its tag), its <b>tasks</b> and their <b>results</b>. Running campaigns show on the <b>Action Calendar</b> under “Campaigns this week”, with the days they run and the tasks due that week.</p><p class="tip">Plan a campaign, then from its page: <b>Add a task</b>, <b>Make a tracked link</b> (the tag is filled in), or <b>Use a launch template</b> to create the whole checklist at once. Link tags used without a campaign are listed on Campaigns so you can turn them into one. Campaigns that ran in a month appear in the Monthly report.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Coverage <small>top bar → Jump to · everyone</small></h3><p>Every brand against every platform (social, ads, shopping, search, email, reviews), with a score per brand. ✓ opens the account; a faded ✓ has no direct link, an amber ring means access hasn’t been checked in 90 days, red means the link is broken. Click <b>+</b> on a gap to make a task (admins can also add the account). <b>Need attention</b> lists accounts to fix.</p><p class="tip">Admins mark a platform a brand doesn’t need as <b>Not needed</b> (it shows —), so the score only counts real gaps. Accounts saved for “All brands” count for every brand.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Ask OmniVEN AI <small>top bar → Ask OmniVEN AI · everyone</small></h3><p>An assistant that reads OmniVEN’s data and answers questions about it: revenue, channels, search, content gaps, reviews, Reddit, AI answers, affiliates, email, campaigns, outreach, site check, coverage, tasks, routines and the team. It looks things up first, compares periods and brands, explains causes, and ends with concrete next steps you can paste in as tasks.</p><p class="tip">Pick a <b>Focus</b> (a section or one brand) to steer it, and ask follow-ups in the same conversation. Deep answers take a minute or two; the page updates by itself. It can only read, never change anything. It sees what you can see: revenue, affiliate and email numbers only if you can see Sales. Sticky notes, Slack messages, logins and keys are never sent. Your conversations are private to you.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>{{ \App\Support\BrandIcon::html('slack', 'xs') }}Morning summary <small>Slack · weekdays</small></h3><p>Each weekday morning OmniVEN sends you a Slack message with your Hotlist, anything overdue or due today, and your routines that are due, with a link back to your board. Days with nothing on your plate are skipped.</p><p class="tip">On the Tasks board, your column says <b>Morning Slack summary: on</b>. Click it to preview today’s message, send it to yourself now, or turn it off.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Launch templates <small>Tasks → From a template</small></h3><p>Ready-made checklists for a <b>Product launch</b> and a <b>Seasonal sale</b>: brief, creative, Klaviyo emails, campaign links, social, CJ and Awin, ads, Merchant Center, launch day and the follow-up. Give it a name (e.g. “Black Friday 2026”), a brand, the start day and who does what, and every step lands on the board with its due date.</p><p class="tip">Tick <b>Skip</b> for steps that don’t apply. Each launch gets its own page showing how many of its tasks are done. Admins add steps or new templates with <b>Edit</b>; write <code>{name}</code> where the launch name goes.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card cal-card"><h3>Action calendar <small>top bar · Q4 plan</small></h3><p>The week-by-week social runbook for 39DollarGlasses, Ocusafe and Neurolux: daily posts, one-off setup jobs, the phase and seasonal angle for each week, the boost log and the Sunday review note.</p><p class="tip">Tick items as you go. Log every boost decision, including skips.</p></article>
        <article class="tab-card"><h3>Access <small>who owns what</small></h3><p>Which login account each tool and brand account lives under, who owns it, and how to get in. Admins also see access requests and broken links here.</p><p class="tip">Need in? Press Request access on that row.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

